2. In ''entom.'', a generic name which has been used for certain beetles and flies, but is in each case preoccupied in ichthyology.

'''trichopathic''' (trik-ō-path′ik), ''a.'' [< ''trichopath-y'' + ''-ic.''] Relating to disease of the hair.

'''trichopathy''' (tri-kop′a-thi), ''n.'' [< Gr. θρίξ (τριχ-), hair, + -πάθεια, < πάθος, suffering.] Treatment of diseases of the hair.

'''Trichophocinæ''' (trik″ō-fō-sī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < Gr. θρίξ (τριχ-), hair, + φώκη, a seal, + ''-inæ''.] A subfamily of the ''Otariidæ'', or eared seals, including the hair-seals as distinguished from the fur-seals (''Ulophocinæ''). There is no type genus.

'''trichophocine''' (trik-ọ-fō′sin), ''a.'' Pertaining to the ''Trichophocinæ'', or having their characters.

'''trichophore''' (trik′ọ-fōr), ''n.'' [< Gr. θρίξ (τριχ-), hair, + -φορος, < φέρειν = E. ''bear''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. In ''bot.'', the special cell or chain of cells in certain algæ which bears the trichogyne. See ''Florideæ''. ''Bennett and Murray'', Cryptog. Bot., p. 199.—2. In ''zoöl.'', a process of the integument of certain annelids, as ''Polychæta'', within which are developed the peculiar chitinous setæ of the parapodia, and which incloses the bases of the pencil-like bundles of setæ (whence the name).

string: see ''cord''<sup>1</sup>, ''chord''.] I. ''n.'' In ''music'', any instrument with three strings, especially the three-stringed lute.

II. ''a.'' Having three strings; characterized by three strings.—'''Trichord pianoforte''', {{smaller|a pianoforte in which most of the digitals have each three strings tuned in unison.}}

'''trichorexis''' (trik-ọ-rek′sis), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. θρίξ (τριχ-), hair, + ῥῆξις, a breaking, < ῥηγνύναι, break.] Brittleness of the hair.—'''Trichorexis nodosa''', {{smaller|a disease of the hair characterized by brittleness and the formation of swellings on the shaft.}}

'''trichorrhea, trichorrhœa''' (trik-ọ-rē′ä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. θρίξ (τριχ-), hair, + ῥοία, a flowing, < ῥεῖν, flow.] Falling of the hair; alopecia.

'''Trichosanthes''' (trik-ō-san′thēz), ''n.'' [NL. (Linnæus, 1737), named from the fringed petals; < Gr. θρίξ (τριχ-), hair, + ἄνθος, flower.] A genus of plants, of the order ''Cucurbitaceæ'' and tribe ''Cucumerineæ''. {{smaller|It is characterized by entire calyx-lobes, a five-parted wheel-shaped fringed corolla, conduplicate anther-cells, and numerous polymorphous seeds. There are about 42 species, natives of tropical Asia, northern Australia, and Polynesia. They are annual or perennial climbers, sometimes with a tuberous root, bearing entire or lobed and cordate leaves and unbranched or forking tendrils. The flowers are white and monœcious—the male racemed, the female solitary—and followed by a fleshy smooth or furrowed fruit, often large and globose, oblong, or conical, sometimes elongated, slender, striped, and serpent-like. ''T. anguina'' and ''T. colubrina'' are known as ''snake-gourd'' or ''viper-gourd'', also as ''snake-cucumber'' (which see, under ''cucumber'').}}

